---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Sessions 17&ndash;20: Concept, Material &amp; CAD</h1>
        <p class="text-sn-theme">The Arcade Cabinet Project &mdash; Milestone 2 of 5</p>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Sessions 17&ndash;20</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Concept, Material &amp; CAD</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>You Have Requirements. Now Design to Them.</h2>
              <p>
                Everything from here forward traces back to your Milestone 1
                requirements summary &mdash; not a fresh idea disconnected
                from your client.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Step 1: Three Genuinely Different Concepts</h2>
              <p>
                Same rule as Unit 1's Session 3: quantity first, no judging
                yet, genuinely different &mdash; not the same cabinet in a
                different color.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Step 2: Score Them</h2>
              <p>
                Decision matrix, same categories as before: Cost,
                Feasibility, Time, and Alignment &mdash; this time, alignment
                with <b class="text-sn-theme">your client's requirements</b>,
                not a hypothetical success criteria.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Step 3: Choose Real Materials</h2>
              <p>
                Session 2 asked you to choose materials for a side panel, a
                control panel, and a back panel &mdash; hypothetically. Now
                do it for real, for your actual chosen concept.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Step 4: Model the Full Cabinet</h2>
              <p>
                Apply Sessions 5&ndash;6's assembly and mate skills &mdash;
                but bigger and more complex than the practice box: a monitor
                opening, a control panel angle, cutouts for buttons and
                joysticks.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Every Joint, Individually Verified</h2>
              <p>
                Session 6's exact rule still applies &mdash; a cabinet that
                looks right from a distance can still hide one bad joint.
                More panels now means more places for that to happen.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">Alignment now means alignment with a real client, not a rubric.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                If your highest-scoring concept doesn't actually answer your
                client's stated need, the matrix isn't done its job &mdash;
                revisit your scoring.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">This model is what Milestone 3 programs toolpaths against.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                A CAD mistake here becomes a CAM problem two sessions from
                now &mdash; the same relationship as Session 6 taught.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Next Up</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">CAM Programming (Sessions 21&ndash;22).</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Turning this real, unique cabinet design into real toolpaths.
              </p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3>Step 1: Sketch 3 Concepts</h3>
          <p>
            Starting from your Milestone 1 requirements, sketch three
            genuinely different cabinet concepts.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 2: Build Your Decision Matrix</h3>
          <p>
            Score each concept on Cost, Feasibility, Time, and Alignment
            with your actual client's requirements. Choose with reasons, not
            a vote.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 3: Choose Your Materials</h3>
          <p>
            For at least your side panels, control panel, and back panel,
            choose a material and justify it using Session 2's reasoning
            &mdash; applied to your real design this time.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 4: Model the Full Assembly</h3>
          <p>
            Build your chosen concept as a full CAD assembly, at real stock
            thickness, with every joint individually verified.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Before Session 20 ends, confirm all of the following:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Three genuinely different concepts were sketched, not 3 variations on one idea</li>
            <li>Your decision matrix has real scores and reasoning, tied to your actual client's requirements</li>
            <li>Each core panel's material is chosen and justified with a real property</li>
            <li>Your full assembly is modeled at real stock thickness with every joint individually verified</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these on your own. Reflections are individual, even when the rest of the session was team work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>What was the hardest part of scaling your CAD skills from Session 6's practice box up to a real cabinet?</li>
            <li>Looking at your finished model, which panel do you expect will be the most complex to program in CAM next milestone?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Submit your 3 concept sketches, your decision matrix, your
            material choices with justifications, your full CAD assembly,
            and your reflection.
          </p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Milestone Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
